% Use, duplication, or disclosure by the Government is subject to
% restrictions as set forth in subparagraph (c)(1)(ii) of the Rights in
% Technical Data and Computer Software clause at DFARS 52.227-7013 and
% in similar clauses in the FAR and NASA FAR Supplement.
%
%              @(#)journal.ps 23.7 91/02/11
% 
% ---------
% 
% This is the new OpenWindows journaler.  It is a (mostly) complete
% rewrite of earlier versions of journaling.  A default user interface
% is included in a separate file.
% 
% The functionality of this journaling package is similar to that
% provided by earlier versions, but improvements include the following:
% 
%   * The journaler is now implemented as a class.  The journaler is
%     structured for easy subclassing, and hooks are provided for easy
%     implementation of most things you might want to do.
% 
%   * The interface to journaling is simpler and more generalized.
% 
%   * Code is simpler and more structured.
% 
%   * It now has comments that tell you what is going on.
% 
%   * Journal scripts are now executable PostScript files.  They do
%     not require outside interpretation and may be simply exec'ed
%     within the context of the journaler, where a number of
%     utilities expected by the scripts are defined.
% 
%   * Mouse motion is handled correctly and uniformly.
% 
%   * During recording, only a specific (but extensible) set of event
%     types are logged.  Previously, a list of event types not to log
%     was maintained and any event not on this list was recorded.
% 
%   * There now exist precisely defined and documented error handling
%     and recovery standards.  The current schema is designed with
%     subclassers and user interface writers in mind.
% 
%   * The journaler is self-cleaning and robust.  No garbage will be
%     left lying around.  Subprocesses are fault-tolerant and support
%     cleanup-on-error.
% 
%   * The journaler is sensitive to the presence of other event
%     logger processes and will not stomp on them, although it can
%     neither play nor record without being the logger.  Note that this
%     means it is not possible to simultaneously record and play.
% 
%   * Synchronous playback now supports a flexible, low-overhead
%     timeout handler.
% 
%   * Bunching of sync events during recording is handled at a higher
%     level than before, so event-writing utilities don't have to
%     worry about it.  Bunching policy is easy to change via
%     subclassing.
% 
%   * Three different synchronization policies are available during
%     playback: strict, loose, and none.  Loose synchronization is the
%     most generally successful of these and is the default.
% 
%   * High-speed playback mode will play back scripts at a speed just
%     slow enough to be sure that each submitted event has a unique time
%     stamp.  This is compatible with all modes of synchronous playback,
%     although in practice it can sometimes cause timing problems with
%     complicated packages such as OpenLook menus.
% 
%   * All options may be changed at any time and take effect "almost
%     immediately".  Currently pending operations (syncs, current
%     event awaiting submission) are typically not affected.  For
%     example, event timing data is now based on deltas rather than
%     absolute times, so changing the playback speed in the middle of
%     a journal is handled correctly.  The one exception is the
%     journal file name.
% 
%   * User aborts are now supported.  Clicking the mouse during
%     playback gracefully terminates the script.  Abort monitoring is
%     performed by a separate process, and is transparent to the rest
%     of the playback code.  Subclassers can redefine playback without
%     explicitly dealing with aborts.
% 
%   * Input events (except /MouseDragged events) generated on playback
%     are recognized by the /JournallingEvent key.
% 
%   * 23% fewer calories than ordinary mayonaise.  Optional designer
%     coat rack available in many areas.  Non-comedogenic.
% 
% 
% Interface methods
% 
% There are only three non-trivial public methods: /record, /play,
% and /stop.  None of them take arguments.
% 
% /record
% 
% record opens the journal file for writing and begins logging events.
% There are four different kinds of records that can be written out:
% one for setting initial conditions, one for recording mouse motion
% events, one for recording other kinds of user input, and one for
% logging synchronization events.  These are detailed below.
% 
%   Calling record during recording is a no-op.
%   Calling record during playback is an error.
% 
% /stop
% 
%   stop halts the journaler in its tracks and cajoles it
%   gracefully back to a cleaned-up, idle state.  It more or less
%   combines the functions "stop recording", "stop playing", "reset
%   journaler", and "stop the world I want to get off".
% 
%   It is safe to call stop at any time.  You may call it as
%   frequently as you like if it makes your application structure
%   simpler.
% 
% /play
% 
%   Starts playing the journal file.
% 
%   Calling play during playback is a no-op.
%   Calling play during recording is an error.
% 
% Options
% 
% A number of recording and playback options are defined.
% 
%   /speed               % -- => speed
%   /setspeed            % speed => --
% 
% The playback speed is a multiplier applied to the spacing between
% events.  Thus, a speed of 1 plays back the journal at the same speed
% at which it was recorded.  A speed of 0.5 plays it back twice as fast,
% and a speed of 2 plays at half speed.
% 
% Submitted events will always have unique timestamps.
% 
% Note that if the speed is set to 0, no time is allowed between events
% except that necessary to insure timestamp uniqueness.  Speed 0 is
% handled as an optimized special case.
% 
% Speed changes take effect no later than the next event played.  The
% current event pending submission (if any) may play at either the old
% or new speed.
% 
%   /synchronization	% -- => policy
%   /setsynchronization % policy => -- 
% 
% Synchronized playback is not always 100% reliable, so three different
% synchronization policies are available to fit a variety of needs:
% /Strict, /Loose, and /None.
% 
% Strict synchronization interprets the script as an inflexible
% blueprint for the sequence in which events occur.  It requires that
% all sync events be caused by caused by the input events that
% immediately precede them.  Strict synchronization is easily broken,
% for example, by moving the mouse while a window is created.
% 
% Loose synchronization assumes only that each synchronization event is
% the result of _some_ user event which precedes it.  As long as the
% results follow the causes in the script, loose synchronization will
% handle the situation with aplomb.  However, loose synchronization will
% become confused if extra events are generated during playback.  For
% example, if an extraneous window is created during playback this is
% sure to mess up the script.
% 
% No synchronization ignores sync points.
% 
% Setting the synchronization policy will take effect "soon", but cannot
% be used to break a synchronization attempt in progress.
% 
%   /journalfile	% -- => string
%   /setjournalfile	% string => --
% 
% Sets or gets the current journal file name.  setjournalfile does not
% take effect until the next play or record.  setjournalfile during
% continuous play is handled correctly (i.e.  setjournalfile does not
% affect it).
% 
%   /playforever?       % -- => bool
%   /setplayforever     % bool => --
% 
% Set or query continuous play mode.  If continuous play mode is set,
% journal scripts are considered infinite loops on playback.  No
% cacheing occurs, so the script file must stay around as long as the
% journal is playing from it.  setplayforever always takes effect
% immediately.
% 
%   /timeout            % -- => time
%   /settimeout         % time => --
% 
% Set or get the timeout value: how long to wait for a sync to complete
% before assuming success.  Does not affect a sync in progress.
% 
%   /journalstate              % -- => state
% 
% Query the state of the journaler.  Currenly, only /Idle, /Playing,
% and /Recording are defined.
% 
% 
% Journaler states and changes of state
%
% The journaler always knows its current state.  Whenever the
% journaler changes state, the ChangeState method is called with the
% new state as an argument.  ChangeState is always called no matter why
% the change occurs.
% 
% The correct cliche for writing a user interface is to subclass
% ChangeState and make all your button highlighting, etc. be tied to the
% information you get via that route.  Do NOT do something like send out
% a play and then disable your play button immediately.
%
% In some cases this may open a small window in which a user could, say,
% press a button twice.  However, such occurrences will be handled
% fairly reasonably (no-op for the same button, error and return to idle
% for different buttons).
% 
% Avoid using the /journalstate method unless absolutely necessary.  If
% you have to call /journalstate, you may not be using the journaling
% package in the most optimal way.
% 
%
% Error handling
% 
% All errors the journaler encounters are considered equally severe.
% When an error is detected, JournalError is called with the name of the
% error.  Errors always force the journal back to the idle state.  It is
% the responsibility of the code that calls JournalError to implement
% this, not JournalError.  Most errors will generate a call to
% ChangeState as well.
% 
% Minimal error handling consists of no error handling.  As long as you
% act on the state change events, you won't get into strange situations.
% 
% Possible errors are:
% 
%   /EventLoggerBusy        % Another process is already logging events
%   /BadJournalFileName     % Couldn't open the journal file
%   /JournalBusy            % Tried to play while recording or vice versa
%   /BadSetSpeedValue       % Non-numeric value for /setspeed
%   /BadSetSyncValue        % Non-boolean given to /setsynchronization
%   /BadSetFileValue        % Non-string given to /setjournalfile
%   /BadSetPlayForever      % Non-boolean given to /setplayforever
%   /BadSetTimeoutValue     % Non-numeric given to /settimeout
%   PostScript errors       % As normal, probably indicates a bug
% 
% 
% Journal file record formats
%
% The first line in a script is always a call to initiallocation, which
% sets the mouse position and cleanses the input queue.  It looks like:
%
%   823 453 initiallocation
%
% A mouse motion record looks like:
% 
%   394 729 0.0003 moveTo
% 
% The first two numbers are the coordinates to move to, and the third
% number is the time delta between this event and the last recorded
% event.  The default implementation of moveTo computes when the event
% should be submitted, sleeps until then, and then calls
% setcursorlocation.  A side effect of setcursorlocation is the
% generation of a /MouseDragged event describing the move.
% 
% A user input event looks like:
% 
%   /RightMouseButton /DownTransition 645 304 0.0183411 userevent
% 
% The userevent procedure takes the five arguments from the stack, packs
% them into a new event, and submits the event at the appropriate time.
% 
% The first two arguments are the Name and Action of the event, and the
% second two are the mouse coordinates.  userevent handles the time delta
% the same way that moveTo does.
% 
% Synchronization events are specified in groups so that slight
% rearrangements of the event sequence do not stymie the player's
% attempt to synchronize.  A typical record looks like:
% 
%   startsyncdata
%       /ExitEvent 2 def
%       /EnterEvent 1 def
%       /DoItEvent 1 def
%   syncpoint
% 
% startsyncdata opens up a new dictionary into which the event counts
% are def'ed.  syncpoint closes the dictionary and monitors incoming
% events until the required number of each listed event have been
% received.
% 
% The default sync timeout is about 10 seconds - probably too low in
% many cases, but a handy value to use until synchronized journaling
% gets really reliable.  The default timeout action is to abort the sync
% and continue playing as if the sync had succeeded.
% 
% 
% Subclasser cliches
% 
% Here are brief instruction for doing some of the less weird things you
% might want to do:
%   
%   To log more event types than normal, override the
%   SubClasserLoggedEvents dictionary and define keys into it.
%   
%   To keep some sync events from being logged (or outlog filter events
%   completely, for that matter), override FilterSyncEvents and chuck
%   out the ones you don't want.  Combines well with
%   SubClasserLoggedEvents to give you a "preview" of special events
%   before having to commit to logging.
%   
%   To use a different script format, override WriteEvent,
%   WriteMoveTo, or FlushSyncEvents, and the playback handlers that go
%   with whatever you change (userevent, moveTo, and
%   startsyncdata/syncpoint).
%   
%   To change the sync timeout behavior, override TimeoutHandler.
%   
%   To synchronize on other criteria than just event name, override
%   CacheSyncEvent and FlushSyncEvents to write out more info, and
%   override MatchSyncEvents.  You will have to override Synchronize and
%   FlushInputQueue as well.
%   
%   To change the kinds of events that cause a user abort during
%   playback, override AbortEvents.  To do something really wild with
%   user aborts, override MonitorUserQuit.  Don't forget that it's a
%   separate process.
%   
%   To provide a new playback timing policy, override WaitPostTime.
%   
%   To hook into option value changes, look at SetOptionValue.
%   
% 
% People who have worked on this:
% 
%   Jerry Farrell, ????
%   Steven Isaac, 8/25/87
%   Nayeem Islam and Bruce Martin, 9/20/88
%   Bruce Martin, 7/8/89
%   Garth Snyder and Bruce Martin, 7/30/89
% 

currentpacking true setpacking
currentautobind true setautobind

/ClassJournal ClassObject

dictbegin

    /SyncPolicy         /Loose def
    /PlaySpeed          1 def
    /JournalFile        (/tmp/NeWS.journal) def
    /PlayForever        false def
    /JournalProcess     null def
    /JournalState       /Idle def
    /Timeout            .17 def

dictend classbegin

    % Class Variables

    % The debugging file.  A small amount of diagnostic info will come
    % here if it isn't null.  Must be a file object.

    /DebugFile              null def

    % UserEventDict is the list of input events that we want to record.
    % These events include all that can be produced by some user action.
    % Manipulating the mouse produces /MouseDragged, for instance
    
    /UserEventDict dictbegin

	/LeftMouseButton    dup def
	/MiddleMouseButton  dup def
	/RightMouseButton   dup def
	/MouseDragged       dup def

	% Keyboard events

	0 1 255 {
	    16#6F00 add dup def
	} for

    dictend def

    % LoggedEvents is the dictionary of events which will be logged for
    % synchronization.  This used to be a dictionary of events not to
    % log, but for various reasons, this is better.  To wit:
    %
    % 1) Any time someone starts generating random internal events for
    %    their own use, that will break synchronized journaling.  For
    %    example, /Tick.  It was apparently not around when the code was
    %    first written and now causes lockups if not specifically
    %    blocked.
    %    
    % 2) The universe of events we are interested in for synchronized
    %    journaling is much smaller than the universe of events we are
    %    not interested in, so dealing with the former is more efficient.
    %
    % 3) We need to get specific about which events we want to sync on.
    %    "Everything but these N events" doesn't seem like the correct
    %    conceptual solution.
    %
    % 4) Subclassing for special purposes somehow seems nicer if you are
    %    adding things to a dictionary rather than taking 'em out.
    %
    % 5) Negative space. Negative thinking. The Wholly Other.  Bad karma.  

    /LoggedEvents dictbegin

	% Cursor enter and exit events (X11 and NeWS)

	/EnterEvent		null def
	/ExitEvent		null def

	% Mapping and unmapping events (X11 only)

	/MapRequest		null def
	/UnmapRequest		null def
	/MapNotify		null def
	/UnmapNotify		null def

	% Focus handling (X11 and NeWS)

	/LoseFocus		null def
	/RestoreFocus		null def
	/AcceptFocus		null def

	% Misc events

	/Obsolete		null def 
	/DoItEvent		null def
	/ClientMessage		null def

	% Events that might work, but who knows:
	%
	%   /ReparentNotify
	%   /Reply (X11?)
	%   /ConfigureRequest (X11)
	%   /Damaged

	% Events that definitely don't work as sync events (for now):
	%
	%   /PropertyNotify (X11)
	%   /ConfigureNotify (X11)

    dictend def

    % SubClasserLoggedEvents is functionally equivalent to LoggedEvents,
    % and both dictionaries are referenced in tandem.
    %
    % The idea is that LoggedEvents will list the core events that
    % everyone will be interested in.  Subclasses can override
    % SubClasserLoggedEvents to add special interests without worrying
    % about disrupting or reiterating the core event set.

    /SubClasserLoggedEvents nulldict def

    % Input cached events is the list of events that will be compiled
    % into the summarized input queue.  It defaults to the contents of
    % LoggedEvents and SubClasserLoggedEvents plus internal events.

    /InputCachedEvents dictbegin

	LoggedEvents		{def} forall
	SubClasserLoggedEvents	{def} forall

	/SyncTimeout null def

    dictend def

    % AbortEvents is a set of events that should cause a playback
    % abort if they are seen during playback.

    /AbortEvents [
	/LeftMouseButton
	/MiddleMouseButton
	/RightMouseButton
    ] def

    % Private methods

    %
    % ChangeState: Change the state of the journaler.  Updates the
    % JournalState instance variable.
    %
    % State information is used to tell what (if any) subprocesses exist
    % that are performing work on behalf of the journaler.  Possible
    % states in the basic journaler are /Recording, /Playing, and /Idle.
    % 
    % The state argument is the name of the state to change to.
    %

    /ChangeState { % state => --

	/JournalState exch store

    } def


    %
    % JournalError: Log a journaling error.  This can be anything, and
    % may be submitted by a subprocess.  The default JournalError
    % prints out the error on currentfile.
    %
    % The argument should be a descriptive name.
    %
    % All user interfaces for journaling should be on the alert for
    % errors and handle them correctly. (easy!)
    %
    % When an error condition is raised, the journaler is _guaranteed_ to
    % clean itself up autonomously and return to the reset /Idle
    % state, even if the error is only an innocuous user input error or
    % some such (e.g. sending a play when the journal is
    % already playing).
    %
    % This is primarily for the convenience of interface writers and
    % subclassers, who reap the benefits of uniform error recovery,
    %
    % Subclassers should preserve the error handling semantics.
    %

    /JournalError { % errorname => --

	/DebugFile self send dup null eq {
	    pop currentprocess /Stderr get
	} if
	exch (Journal error: %\n) fprintf

    } def


    %
    % Set a signal to external clients that we are journaling.
    % 
    % This is used by X clients who want to generate extra
    % synchronization events (ClientMessage) to help.
    %

    /SetJournalingSignal { % -- => --

	version cvr 2.1 ge {
	    Xcompat_private begin   % set up atom and prop
		framebuffer
		/JOURNAL_SYNC false XInternAtom
		()
		/INTEGER false XInternAtom
		8 xsetproperty
	    end
	} {
	    Xcompat_private begin
		(JOURNAL_SYNC) null XInternAtom
		/atom_number exch store
		framebuffer /JOURNAL_SYNC
		/INTEGER % find XA_INTEGER atom
		/Format32 () /ModeReplace XChangeProperty   % data ()
	    end
	} ifelse
	
    } def


    %
    % Remove the journaling signal
    %

    /DeleteJournalingSignal { % -- => --
    
	version cvr 2.1 ge {
	    Xcompat_private begin
		framebuffer
		/JOURNAL_SYNC false XInternAtom
		xdeleteproperty
	    end
	} {
	    Xcompat_private begin % remove property
		framebuffer /JOURNAL_SYNC XDeleteProperty
	    end
	} ifelse
	
    } def



    %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
    
    % Recording code
    
    %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

    % Control functions

    %
    % StartRecorder: Record a journal.
    %
    % StartRecorder calls InitRecorder to get everything set up, then
    % calls RecordLoop (an infinite loop) to do all the actual
    % recording.  When RecordLoop terminates (in the expected case, via
    % a killprocess submitted by stop), the error dictionary is
    % checked to see why.  If RecordLoop terminates because of a true
    % error, this error is passed on to JournalError.  In any case,
    % CleanupRecorder is called to swab the decks.
    %
    % The argument should be a file object opened for writing; it is
    % passed on to InitRecorder.
    %
    % StartRecorder is expected to be executed in subprocess and in an
    % idle context.  Thus running off the end of StartRecorder
    % effectively terminates the recording thread.
    %

    /StartRecorder { % file => --

	geteventlogger null ne {
	    /EventLoggerBusy /JournalError self send
	    stop
	} if

	/InitRecorder self send % --
	
	{/RecordLoop self send} stopped {
	    $error begin
		$error errorname /killprocess ne {
		    errorname /JournalError self send 
		} if
	    end
	} if

	/CleanupRecorder self send
	
    } def


    %
    % InitRecorder: Do all necessary initialization in preparation for
    % recording.  InitRecorder can assume that the journaler is in a
    % cleaned-up and idle state.
    %
    % The argument is a file object opened for writing to which the
    % journal should be written.
    %
    % InitRecorder performs the following functions:
    %
    %   1) Installs itself as the current journaling background
    %      process, in a new process group.
    %
    %   2) Promotes and initializes a number of variables used during
    %      recording.
    %
    %   3) Changes the state of the journaler to /Recording.
    %
    %   4)  Sets up the current process as the system event logger.
    %
    %   5) Sets up the X journaling signal.
    %
    %   6) Writes out the initiallocation record for the script.
    %

    /InitRecorder { % file => --
    
	/JournalProcess currentprocess store
	JournalProcess /Priority SystemPriority put
	JournalProcess /ProcessName (Journal Recorder) put

	/Script         exch promote
	/LastLogTime	currenttime promote
	/LastSerial     0 promote
	/LastTimeStamp  0 promote
	/SyncBuffer     growabledict promote

	/Recording /ChangeState self send

	newprocessgroup

	createevent dup /Name /Fake put expressinterest
	currentprocess seteventlogger

	/SetJournalingSignal self send

	% Make sure we use the right coordinate system

	framebuffer setcanvas
	initmatrix

	% Initialize the cursor loc.

	Script (% % initiallocation\n) [ currentcursorlocation ] fprintf

    } def
    

    %
    % CleanupRecorder: Undo the effects of an InitRecorder.
    %
    % CleanupRecorder calls FlushSyncEvents once to make sure any queued
    % but unlogged sync events get written out, closes the journal file,
    % disposes of all the variables installed by InitRecorder for use
    % during recording, resets system event logging, resets the
    % state of the journaler to idle, removes the instance's reference
    % to the recording process, and deletes the X journal flag.
    %

    /CleanupRecorder { % -- => --
    
	/FlushSyncEvents self send

	Script flushfile % REMIND: I don't think this is needed -grs
	Script closefile
	
	null seteventlogger

	/Script         unpromote
	/LastLogTime	unpromote
	/LastSerial     unpromote
	/LastTimeStamp  unpromote
	/SyncBuffer     unpromote
	
	/JournalProcess null store
	/Idle /ChangeState self send

	/DeleteJournalingSignal self send
	
    } def


    % Actual recording functions and utilities

    % 
    % RecordLoop is the main loop for event logging.
    %
    % RecordLoop examines all events as they come in and determines
    % whether or not to log them.  The rules are:
    %
    %   If the event is a /MouseDragged, the event is logged using the
    %   WriteMoveTo method.  This is because on playback you normally
    %   want to mimic mouse drags by doing a setcursorlocation rather
    %   than posting an actual event.
    %
    %   OTHERWISE, if the event's name is known in UserEventDict, then
    %   the event is considered a generic input event and is written
    %   out with WriteEvent.
    %
    %   OTHERWISE, if the event's name is known in either the LoggedEvents
    %   or SubClasserLoggedEvents dictionaries, it is given to
    %   FilterSyncEvent.  If FilterSyncEvent OK's it, it is passed on to
    %   CacheSyncEvent, which will squirrel it away pending a
    %   FlushSyncEvents.  CacheSyncEvent may be overridden to discard the
    %   event or write it out immediately if desired.
    %
    %   OTHERWISE, the event is discarded.
    %
    % Before calling WriteEvent or WriteMoveTo, RecordLoop always calls
    % FlushSyncEvents.  CleanupRecorder is responsible for insuring that
    % FlushSyncEvents is called once before the journal file is closed.
    %
    % Typically, you want to gather up all your sync events and sync on
    % them together.  CacheSyncEvent and FlushSyncEvents make this easy
    % to do.  Having CacheSyncEvent do all the writing on an
    % event-by-event basis and making FlushSyncEvents a no-op is fine,
    % if that's what you need to do.
    %
    % Since it is implemented as an infinite loop, RecordLoop can never
    % exit except by generating an error or being zapped by a
    % killprocess.
    %

    /RecordLoop { % -- => --

	/LastLogTime currenttime store

	{ % loop

	    awaitevent

	    % We shun events that have Serial numbers in the past (events
	    % that are being redistributed, for instance, by grabs). 

	    dup /Serial get /LastSerial self send gt {		% event

		dup /Name get					% event Name
		
		/UserEventDict self send 1 index known {	% event Name
	    
		    % It's an input event

		    /FlushSyncEvents self send
	        
		    dup /MouseDragged eq {
			1 index /WriteMoveTo self send
		    }{
			1 index /WriteEvent self send
		    } ifelse
	        
		} if					% event Name

		/LoggedEvents self send
		1 index known exch			% event known? name
		/SubClasserLoggedEvents self send
		exch known or {				% event name
		    
		    % It's a sync event

		    dup /EnterEvent eq
		    1 index /ExitEvent eq
		    or {
			1 index /WriteMoveTo self send
		    } if

		    1 index /FilterSyncEvent self send {
			1 index /CacheSyncEvent self send
		    } if
			
		} if					% event name
		
		pop
		dup /Serial get /LastSerial exch store
		/TimeStamp get /LastTimeStamp exch store
	    
	    }{pop} ifelse
	        
	} loop
	
    } def


    %
    % FilterSyncEvent: possibly avoid logging a sync event
    %
    % This is subclasser hook that lets you reject events before
    % CacheSyncEvent sees them.
    %
    % A true return indicates that the event should be logged.
    %

    /FilterSyncEvent { % event => bool

	pop true

    } def
    

    %
    % CacheSyncEvent: handle a sync event.
    %
    % See RecordLoop for a description of how this method is used: an
    % unmodified event is the only argument.
    %
    % This default implementation counts the number of times that each
    % kind of event appears.  It depends on FlushSyncEvents to do all of
    % its writing.
    %
    % Implementations of CacheSyncEvent which require storage should use
    % and depend upon the predefined SyncBuffer dictionary.  Creation of
    % other temporary variables is not recommended, as no facility
    % exists to give a clean-up call when recording is shut down.
    %
    
    /CacheSyncEvent { % event => --
	
	/Name get

	/SyncBuffer self send 1 index known {
	    /SyncBuffer self send 1 index get 1 add
	}{
	    1
	} ifelse

	/SyncBuffer self send 3 1 roll put
	
    } def


    %
    % FlushSyncEvents: write out information about any events queued
    % with CacheSyncEvent.
    %
    % FlushSyncEvents is called at the boundary which separates a number
    % of sync events from an input event.  It should write out any sync
    % events in its queue and clean out the queue so that these events
    % don't make an unwelcome second appearance later on.
    %
    % FlushSyncEvents is guaranteed to be called as the journal
    % terminates, so there is no danger of losing events in the cache.
    %
    % Anything that FlushSyncEvents wants to write in the journal should
    % be sent to the file object Script.  Be aware of and obey, as much
    % as possible, the conventions for logging sync events listed at
    % the head of this file when subclassing this method.
    %
    
    /FlushSyncEvents { % -- => --

	/SyncBuffer self send length 0 gt {
	
	    /Script self send
	    dup dup (startsyncdata\n) writestring % file file

	    /SyncBuffer self send { % file file name #instances
		exch (\t/% % def\n) fprintf
		dup % file file
	    } forall pop % file
	    
	    (syncpoint\n) writestring
	    /SyncBuffer self send cleanoutdict
	
	} if
	
    } def


    %
    % WriteEvent: Output a generic input event to the journal.
    %
    % The default implementation of WriteEvent logs the Name, Action,
    % XLocation, YLocation, and time of the event.
    %
    % Since the set of possible input events is well-characterized,
    % there should be no reason to override this method (unless you
    % want create a journaler that only records sync events or
    % something weird like that).
    %

    /WriteEvent { % event => --
    
	% Do our self sends before we get into the local dict so
	% that we get compiled.

	/LastLogTime self send
	/Script self send % event lastlogtime file 

	 3 -1 roll begin % lastlogtime file
 
	    dup Name writeobject  
	    dup ( ) writestring
	    dup Action writeobject
	    exch TimeStamp exch sub 0 max % file deltatime
	    YLocation
	    XLocation
	    ( % % % userevent\n) fprintf

	    /LastLogTime TimeStamp store
	    
	end
	
    } def


    %
    % WriteMoveTo: Record a mouse motion event.
    %
    % Mouse motion is implemented separately from generic user input.
    % When a /MouseDragged event is submitted by a random process, the
    % cursor location is not properly updated, although the cursor does
    % move on the screen.
    %
    % To make things work right, we need to substitute calls to
    % setcursorlocation for /MouseDragged events when we play back the
    % journal.  The playback-time "moveTo" procedure is expected to wait
    % for the right moment and then simply execute setcursorlocation.
    %

    /WriteMoveTo { % event => --
    
	/Script self send
	/LastLogTime self send % event file logtime
	
	3 -1 roll begin % file logtime
	
	    TimeStamp exch sub 0 max % file deltatime
	    YLocation
	    XLocation
	    (% % % moveTo\n) fprintf

	    /LastLogTime TimeStamp store
	    
	end
	
    } def


    %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

    % Playback code

    %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

    %
    % WaitPostTime: Wait until the right time to submit an event, taking
    % the current PlaySpeed into account.
    %
    % If PlaySpeed is 0, then events are pumped out as fast as
    % possible, subject only to timestamp uniqueness considerations.
    %

    /WaitPostTime { % deltatime => -
    
	/PlaySpeed self send 0 eq {
	
	    pop 

	    /LastPostTime /LastPostTime self send % /LPT LPT
	    currenttime exch 1 index eq {
		{dup currenttime ne {pop currenttime exit} if} loop
	    } if
	    
	    store
	    
	}{

	    /PlaySpeed self send mul % deltatime

	    /LastPostTime self send exch 1 index add % LPT delta+LPT
	    currenttime sub 0 max sleep % LPT
	    
	    currenttime exch 1 index eq {
		{dup currenttime ne {pop currenttime exit} if} loop
	    } if

	    /LastPostTime exch store
	    
	} ifelse
	
    } def


    %
    % FlushInputQueue: clean out the input event summary.
    %

    /FlushInputQueue { % -- => --

	/InputQueue self send cleanoutdict
	countinputqueue {awaitevent pop} repeat

    } def
		    

    %
    % Synchronize: wait until all expected sync events have materialized
    % or hang forever.
    %
    % This method is the meat of synchronized journaling.  Any subclass
    % which wants to do snazzy synchronization will override this.  This
    % default implementation takes a dictionary in which the keys are
    % event names and the values are the number of events of this type
    % to wait for.  Actual event matching is done by MatchSyncEvents.
    %
    % This code doesn't care in what order the events materialize.
    %
    % Events which aren't needed now but which might prove useful later
    % are kept in the cache if the synchronization policy is /Loose.
    %
    
    /Synchronize { % eventdict => -
    
	% First check the cached input events queue.  Maybe we can
	% satisfy the whole sync with summarized events...

	dup /InputQueue self send /MatchSyncEvents self send % eventdict
	
	dup length 0 eq {
	    pop
	}{

	    %  Sigh.  Have to start scanning the input queue.
	    %
	    %  First, put a mark on the stack to aid error recovery.

	    mark exch % mark dict

	    % Submit a postdated event that will let us know when we've
	    % timed out.

	    /Timeout self send currenttime add
	    createevent begin
	
		/Name       /SyncTimeout def
		/TimeStamp  exch def
	    
	    currentdict end dup sendevent exch % mark event dict

	    {{ % inner=loop, outer=stopped - event dict

		pause awaitevent
		
		/Name get dup /SyncTimeout eq { % mark event dict name
		    pop /TimeoutHandler self send
		}{
		    2 copy known { % Sync on this event?
			2 copy get dup 1 eq { % mark event dict name num
			    pop 1 index % mark event dict name dict
			    exch undef dup length 0 eq {exit} if
			}{ % mark event dict name num
			    1 sub 2 index 3 1 roll put
			} ifelse
		    }{ % mark event dict name
			/InputCachedEvents self send 1 index known {
			    /InputQueue self send exch % mark event dict iq name
			    2 copy known { 
				2 copy get 1 add % mark event dict iq name num+1
				put % mark event dict
			    }{
				1 put
			    } ifelse
			}{pop} ifelse
		    } ifelse % mark event dict
		} ifelse % timeout

	    } loop } stopped { % mark dict (timeout), mark event dict (no error)
 	
		 $error begin
		     errorname /timeout eq {
			 pop pop
		    }{
			% Clean off the stack.  To be absolutely sure we
			% don't leave any outstanding SyncTimeout events,
			% we have to comb the stack looking for them, down
			% to the mark. 

			counttomark { % repeat
			    dup type /eventtype eq {
				dup /Name get /SyncTimeout eq
				{recallevent}{pop} ifelse
			    }{pop} ifelse
			} repeat pop
			false errorname assert
		    } ifelse
		end
	    
	    }{ % mark event dict
	
		pop recallevent pop
	    
	    } ifelse % stopped

	} ifelse % zero length

    } def


    %
    % MatchSyncEvents: match the events we are currently syncing on
    % to those in the current (summarized) input queue.
    %
    % The default just compares the names of the events.
    %
    % This is actually general bi-dictionarial redundancy eliminator =:0
    %

    /MatchSyncEvents { % syncdict refdict => --
    
	% We want to cycle through the sync dict and check to see if
	% each key is also listed in the reference dict.  Since we can't
	% simultaneously forall over the sync dict and modify it, we have
	% to precompute an array of all the key names and forall over that.

	exch dup [ exch {pop} forall ] { % forall

	    2 copy get % refdict syncdict syncname synccount
	    3 index 2 index known { % is syncname in refdict?
		3 index 2 index get % rd sd sn sc rc
		2 copy gt {
		    sub % rd sd sn diff
		    3 index 2 index undef % take syncname out of refdict
		    2 index 3 1 roll put % update sync dict
		}{
		    2 copy lt {
			exch sub % rd sd sn diff
			2 index 2 index undef % take out of syncdict
			3 index 3 1 roll put % update refdict
		    }{
			pop pop % rd sd sn 
			2 copy undef % take it out of both dicts
			2 index exch undef
		    } ifelse
		} ifelse
	    }{pop pop} ifelse

	} forall pop pop

    } def
	    

    %
    % TimeoutHandler: What to do if you time out while waiting for a
    % sync event.
    %
    % The eventdict passed in is the current synchronization dictionary.
    % Using the default sync schema, things in this dictionary have
    % not yet been synced on.
    %
    % This is a subclasser hook.  The default just stops with a timeout
    % which is trapped back in Synchronize.  However, it is perfectly
    % legit for this method not to exit.  In this case, the sync
    % continues normally.  Thus, making TimeoutHandler a no op prevents
    % timing out of synchronizations.
    %
    % You can also do a createevent and submit another postdated
    % /SyncTimeout event to buy more time.  TimeoutHandler will be
    % called when the event arrives.
    %
    
    /TimeoutHandler { % timeoutevent eventdict => eventdict

	exch pop % eventdict
	
	/DebugFile self send dup null ne {
	    dup (Sync failed: ) fprintf % dict file
	    dup 2 index {exch (% % ) fprintf dup} forall % dict file file
	    (\n) fprintf flushfile
	}{pop} ifelse

	/FlushInputQueue self send
	false /timeout assert
	
    } def


    %
    % MonitorUserQuit: Playback abort server
    %
    % This method is executed from within a forked context by InitPlayer.
    % It checks to be sure that the user doesn't generate any of the events
    % in the AbortEvents dictionary while a playback is in progress.
    %
    % MonitorUserQuit distinguishes synthetic events submitted by the
    % playback code from genuine user inputs by checking to see if the
    % key /JournallingEvent is defined in the event.
    %

    /MonitorUserQuit { % parentproc => --

	newprocessgroup

	createevent dup /Name AbortEvents put expressinterest

	{
	    awaitevent /JournallingEvent known not {
		/stop self send
	    } if
	} loop
	    
    } def

    
    %
    % StartPlayer: Start playing the journal file.
    %
    % This method should only be called when the journaler is idle, and
    % should be called from a forked process.  StartPlayer essentially
    % just sets everything up, execs the journal file directly, and
    % cleans up the aftermath.
    %
    % If PlayForever is true, the journaler re-executes the file until
    % PlayForever is no longer true.  The file is re-opened on every
    % pass, so if it is deleted, an error results.
    %
    
    /StartPlayer { % -- => --

	geteventlogger null ne {
	    /EventLoggerBusy /JournalError self send
	    stop
	} if

	/InitPlayer self send

	{ % stopped
    
	    % Get the value of JournalFile at the very beginning and
	    % re-use it if we do a PlayForever in case someone changes
	    % the value on us later.
	    
	    /JournalFile self send
    
	    /Playing /ChangeState self send
    
	    { % loop

		/InputQueue self send cleanoutdict

		dup (r) {file} stopped {
		    /BadJournalFileName /JournalError self send
		    pop pop exit
		}{
		    cvx exec
		} ifelse

    		/PlayForever self send not {exit} if
		
	    } loop
	    
	    pop
    
	} stopped {

	% [for testing only]
	% console $error writeobject console flushfile

	    $error begin
	 	errorname /killprocess ne {
	 	    errorname /JournalError self send
	 	} if
	    end
	     
	} if 

	/CleanupPlayer self send
	
    } def


    %
    % InitPlayer: Set up for playing.
    %
    % Very similar to InitRecorder - see that method for explanations.
    %

    /InitPlayer { % -- => --

	/JournalProcess currentprocess store
	JournalProcess /Priority SystemPriority put
	JournalProcess /ProcessName (Journal Player) put

	/LastPostTime       0 promote
	/InputQueue	    growabledict promote
	/Repeat             /repetition ClassRepeatKeys send promote
	false /setrepetition ClassRepeatKeys send
    
	newprocessgroup

	createevent dup /Name /Fake put expressinterest
	currentprocess seteventlogger

	% Create an abort monitor.

	currentprocess { /MonitorUserQuit self send } fork exch pop
	dup /MonitorProc exch promote
	dup /ProcessName (Journal Abort Server) put
	/Priority SystemPriority put

	/SetJournalingSignal self send

    } def


    %
    % CleanupPlayer: reverse the effects of InitPlayer.
    %
    
    /CleanupPlayer { % -- => --

	% Toast the subprocesses

	/MonitorProc self send killprocess

	/MonitorProc		unpromote
	/LastPostTime		unpromote
	/InputQueue		unpromote
	Repeat /setrepetition ClassRepeatKeys send
	/Repeat			unpromote
	
	null seteventlogger
	/JournalProcess null store
	/Idle /ChangeState self send

	/DeleteJournalingSignal self send

    } def


    % Public methods

    /destroy {
    
	/stop self send
	/destroy super send
	
    } def

    %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

    % Playback utility code

    %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%


    %
    % initiallocation: start the playback process at this location
    %
    % This utility is called from the journal script at the start of the
    % playback.  It primes the event system by setting the cursor location,
    % and cleaning out any extraneous events that may be generated by this
    % action.
    %

    /initiallocation { % x y => --
    
	framebuffer setcanvas initmatrix
	setcursorlocation

	{
	    pause
	    countinputqueue dup 0 eq {
		pop exit
	    } {
		{ awaitevent pop } repeat
	    } ifelse
	} loop

	/LastPostTime currenttime store

    } def


    %
    % moveTo: move the cursor
    %
    % This is a utility called directly from journal scripts.  It is
    % the input counterpart to WriteMoveTo.
    %

    /moveTo {  % x y time => --
    
	/WaitPostTime self send
	setcursorlocation pause
	
    } def


    %
    % userevent: submit an input event.
    %
    % This function is called directly from journal scripts.  It is the
    % input counterpart to WriteEvent.
    %
    % userevent cobbles together an event from the info on the stack and
    % submits it.
    %
    
    /userevent { % name action x y time => --
    
	/WaitPostTime self send
	
	createevent begin

	    /YLocation      exch def
	    /XLocation      exch def
	    /Action         exch def
	    /Name           exch def
	    
	    /TimeStamp      currenttime def
	    /JournallingEvent true def
	    
	currentdict end sendevent pause
	
    } def


    %
    % startsyncdata: create something to hold sync events
    %
    % A journal script calls this method before a run of sync events
    % comes down the line.  See the beginning of this file for more info
    % on how this works.  This will probably need to be overridden if
    % you change the format of sync events too much.
    %
    % In the default case, sync event records just def numbers into 
    % names, so all we need to do is be sure there's a dict there to
    % catch them.
    %
    
    /startsyncdata { % -- => --
    
	dictbegin
	
    } def


    %
    % syncpoint: do a synchronization if necessary
    %
    % This closes up a run of sync events started with startsyncdata and
    % synchronizes on them if necessary.
    %
    
    /syncpoint { % -- => --
    
	dictend % dict
	
	/SyncPolicy self send { % case

	    /Strict {
		/Synchronize self send
		/FlushInputQueue self send
	    }

	    /Loose {
		/Synchronize self send
	    }

	    /None {
		pop pause
		/FlushInputQueue self send
	    }
	    
	} case

    } def


    %
    % SetOptionValue: set the value of one of the instance variables.
    %
    % This is just a utility used to make the set/get option public
    % routines smaller.  It checks to be sure the proposed value is
    % of the right type.  If not, it calls JournalError to distribute
    % the given error and calls stop to reset the journaler.
    % Otherwise, it sets the value of the variable.
    %   

    /SetOptionValue { % value errname variable type => --

	% Reals are more general than ints and we don't really need
	% ints anywhere.  "Converting" ints to reals makes the type check
	% simple for numbers.

	3 index type dup /integertype eq {pop /realtype} if 

	ne { % value errname variable
	    pop /JournalError self send
	    /stop self send
	    pop
	}{
	    2 index store pop pop
	} ifelse

    } def
	

    %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

    % Public interface methods

    %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
    
    %
    % record: Start recording.
    %
    % If event security is on, print error message
    %
    % If the journaler is playing, record is an error.
    % If the journaler is recording, record is a no-op.
    %

    /record { % -- => --
    
	createevent /Synthetic get { 
	    /SecureMode /JournalError self send
	} {
	    /JournalState self send {

		/Playing {
		    /stop self send
		    /JournalBusy /JournalError self send
		}

		/Idle {
		    /JournalFile self send (w) {file} stopped {
			pop pop
			/BadJournalFileName /JournalError self send
		    }{
			{
			    /StartRecorder self send
			} fork pop
			pop
		    } ifelse
		}
		
	    } case
	} ifelse
    } def


    %
    % play: Play a previously recorded script.
    %
    % If event security is on, print error message
    %
    % If the journaler is recording, play is an error.
    % If the journaler is playing, play is a no-op.
    %
    
    /play { % -- => --

	createevent /Synthetic get { 
	    /SecureMode /JournalError self send
	} {
	    /JournalState self send {

		/Recording {
		    /stop self send
		    /JournalBusy /JournalError self send
		}

		/Idle {
		    {
			/StartPlayer self send
		    } fork pop
		}
		
	    } case
	} ifelse
    } def


    %
    % stop: Stop recording or playing or whatever it is the
    % journaler is doing.  Go back to cleaned-up idle state.
    %
    % It is ok (no-op) to call stop on an idle journaler.
    %
    % Since both the player code and the recorder code are prepared
    % to deal with being killed and clean themselves up, all we
    % need to do is kill the current process.
    %

    /stop { % -- => --
    
	/JournalState self send /Idle ne {
	    /JournalProcess self send killprocess
	} if 
	
    } def


    % Trivial option query/set routines

    %
    % speed and setspeed: query or set the playing speed
    %

    /speed { % -- => speed
	/PlaySpeed self send
    } def

    /setspeed { % speed => -
	/BadSetSpeedValue /PlaySpeed /realtype
	/SetOptionValue self send
    } def

    %
    % synchronization and setsynchronization: get or set the sync
    % policy.
    %

    /synchronization { % -- => policy
	/SyncPolicy self send
    } def

    /setsynchronization { % policy => --
    
	dup {

	    /None /Loose /Strict {
		/SyncPolicy exch store
	    }

	    /Default {
		pop
		/BadSetSyncValue /JournalError self send
	    }
	    
	} case
	
    } def

    %
    % journalfile and setjournalfile: get or set the journal filename
    %

    /journalfile { % -- => filename
	/JournalFile self send
    } def

    /setjournalfile { % filename => --
	/BadSetFileValue /JournalFile /stringtype
	/SetOptionValue self send
    } def

    %
    % playforever setplayforever: get or set loop playback mode
    %

    /playforever? { % -- => bool
	/PlayForever self send
    } def

    /setplayforever { % bool => --
	/BadSetPlayForeverValue /PlayForever /booleantype
	/SetOptionValue self send
    } def

    %
    % timeout settimeout: get or set syncrhonous playback timeout value
    %
    
    /timeout { % -- => time
	/Timeout self send
    } def

    /settimeout { % time => --
	/BadSetTimeoutValue /Timeout /realtype
	/SetOptionValue self send
    } def

    %
    % journalstate: query state: /Idle, /Playing, or /Recording
    %

    /journalstate { % -- => state
	/JournalState self send
    } def
    
classend def

setautobind setpacking
